      "Before Socrates, Greek philosophy was *cosmology*: the study of what the world is fundamentally made of, and how it works. The thinkers we now call the **pre-Socratics** (the name itself measures everything against him) were trying to find the one underlying stuff or principle behind the changing surface of things. Thales said water, Anaximenes said air, Heraclitus said everything is fire and flux, the atomists said tiny uncuttable bodies moving in the void (the Greeks chapter). These were not foolish guesses; they were the first attempts in the West to explain nature by nature itself rather than by the doings of gods, and that is a genuine intellectual revolution. But their question pointed *outward*, at the heavens and the elements. How a person ought to live was not really on the menu.",
