          "p": "What can we say about the man behind the three portraits with some confidence? He was born in Athens around 470 BC, in a district called Alopece, to a father, Sophroniscus, who worked stone (later legend, traced to the biographer Diogenes Laertius six centuries on, upgrades him to a \"sculptor\" and has young Socrates carving statues, but the plainer stoneworker reading is safer) and a mother, Phaenarete, who was a midwife. Socrates himself uses that detail, in Plato's *Theaetetus*, for one of his best self-descriptions: he says he practices his mother's trade on the mind, helping others give birth to ideas while himself producing none. He married a woman named Xanthippe, who later tradition turned into the archetypal shrewish wife, though that caricature is mostly the work of much-later anecdote; the contemporary Xenophon presents her quite differently. In his *Symposium* he has Socrates explain that he chose a difficult partner on purpose, reasoning that if he could learn to get along with *her*, he could get along with anyone at all. They had three sons. And he was, by every account, strikingly ugly (snub-nosed, thick-lipped, bald, pop-eyed), a fact his friends teased him about and he cheerfully agreed with."
