          "p": "The clearest way into the elenchus is to run one, move by move. Take the dialogue called the *Euthyphro*. The setting is loaded: Socrates is on his way to court to face his own impiety charge, and he runs into a young man named Euthyphro who is, of all things, prosecuting his *own father* for the death of a hired worker. Euthyphro is supremely confident this is the pious thing to do, so confident he's hauling his own father into court over it. Socrates, who is about to be tried for *impiety*, pounces: wonderful, here's an expert on piety exactly when I need one. Teach me. What *is* piety? Define it, so I can hold up my own conduct against the standard."
