          "p": "**Euthyphro's first answer:** piety is what *I'm doing right now*, prosecuting a wrongdoer, no matter who he is. Socrates blocks this immediately, and the block is itself one of his most important moves. You've given me an *example*, he says, not a *definition*. I didn't ask which actions are pious; I asked what piety *itself* is, the single thing that all pious actions have in common, the standard by which we *call* them pious in the first place. Without that, you can't actually tell whether prosecuting your father is pious; you'd be assuming the very thing in question. (This is one of Socrates' genuine philosophical contributions, the one Aristotle later credits him with: the demand for the *universal*, the \"what is it?\" question, the insistence that you must know what justice *is*, in general, before you can judge whether any particular act is just. Examples can't answer the question; they presuppose its answer.)"
